<Layout title="How the fair picker works — Who Goes First?" description="Each listed player gets an equal chance, with secure browser randomness and an outcome locked before any reveal begins."><div class="content-page prose"><h1>How the draw works</h1><p class="lede">With four players, each person has a 1 in 4 chance. With twelve, it’s 1 in 12.</p><h2>Around the table</h2><p>Seat 1 is the person holding the phone; count clockwise. Tap any player name to change it, or paste a list. Blank names keep their seat number. Adding or removing seats keeps the remaining names in place.</p><p>Choose Quick or Instant for a fast answer, or try <a href="/methods/spinner/">Spinner</a>, <a href="/methods/cards/">Card Draw</a>, <a href="/methods/balloon/">Balloon Rise</a> or <a href="/methods/towers/">Towers</a>. You can also try <a href="/methods/straws/">Shortest Match</a>, try a <a href="/methods/dice/">Dice Roll</a>, or run a <a href="/methods/race/">Marble Race</a>. Every method uses the same equal-chance draw.</p><h2>The decision comes first</h2><p>When you pick, we freeze the current player list and ask your browser’s secure random-number generator for a value. Rejection sampling discards the small leftover range that would otherwise favor some positions. Every listed entry gets the same number of possible values.</p><p>The result is locked before an animation begins. Balloon height, names, colors, sound, screen size, and the “Show result now” button cannot change it.</p><h2>Repeats are normal</h2><p>Each pick is independent. The same person can win twice—or more—in a row. We don’t quietly remove the last winner or balance results over time. Duplicate names are separate entries with identifying numbers.</p><h2>If something interrupts</h2><p>Switching tabs reveals the already-selected winner. If sound or a decorative effect fails, the text result still appears. If secure randomness fails, we show an error and select nobody; we don’t substitute a weaker generator.</p><h2>House rules work differently</h2><p><a href="/house-rules/">Fun questions</a> do not give everyone equal chances of qualifying. Agree as a group, skip the question, or use the fair picker as an explicit tie-break.</p><p>This is a casual game-night utility. It has not been independently audited or certified for regulated prize draws.</p><a class="inline-link" href="/">Pick a starting player</a></div></Layout>

